<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gaius Valerius Triarius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gaius Valerius Triarius was a Roman legate under Lucullus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-mithridatic-war">Third Mithridatic War</a>. In 67 BCE, while Lucullus was away, he attacked Mithridates VI near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a> in Pontus and suffered a heavy defeat in which, according to ancient writers, thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and many officers died. The disaster allowed Mithridates to recover much of his kingdom.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Triarius belonged to the gens Valeria, though unlike its best-known members the Triarii were plebeians. Their cognomen may recall military service, since the triarii were the third and most veteran line of the Republican legion. His father was also named Gaius, and his brother Lucius Valerius Triarius was quaestor in 81 and praetor in 78 BCE. He married Flaminia, a friend of Servilia, the half-sister of Cato and mother of Brutus.</p><p>They had two sons. Gaius sided against Caesar in the civil war, commanded a flotilla from the province of Asia in the Adriatic in 48 BCE and advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> at Pharsalus to hold his infantry still while the Caesarians charged; Publius prosecuted Marcus Aemilius Scaurus for corruption in 54 BCE. He may also have been the father of Paula Valeria, who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimus-junius-brutus-albinus">Decimus Junius Brutus Albinus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 72 BCE, after defeating the fleet of Mithridates VI at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-licinius-lucullus">Lucius Licinius Lucullus</a> gave Triarius command of his fleet on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>. Triarius joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius-cotta">Marcus Aurelius Cotta</a> at the siege of Heraclea Pontica. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/machares">Machares</a>, son of Mithridates and ruler of the Bosporian Kingdom, stopped supplying the city, the garrison commander Connacorex, who trusted Triarius more than Cotta, negotiated with him and slipped away by sea through his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. Triarius then took the city, and his troops looted it, nearly provoking a fight with Cotta's men, who felt cheated of the glory and plunder.</p><p>In the spring of 67 BCE Mithridates returned to Pontus while Lucullus was besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>. He routed the legate Marcus Fabius Hadrianus, and Triarius, nearby with reinforcements, took command of both forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a> and Appian say he wished to win before Lucullus arrived and took the glory, though this is disputed. He camped near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a>, and after a storm in the night, which both sides took as an omen, the armies met on the plain.</p><p>Mithridates broke one part of the Roman line, then sent his cavalry round the rest, and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> back on a flooded trench he had hidden, where many were cut down. Triarius escaped, leaving 7,000 dead, among them 24 tribunes and 150 centurions, and Lucullus had to hide him from his own soldiers, who wanted to lynch him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gaius Valerius Triarius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ancient Rome, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-35" aria-label="Ancient Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gaius Valerius Triarius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=-35">What was here in 35 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
